{
  "dom_id": "step-q2611",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $6x+3y=3$ then $y=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate the variable $y.$ To do this, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $6x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+3y & =3 \\\\ 6x+3y-6x & =3-6x \\\\ 6x-6x+3y & =3-6x \\\\ 3y & =3-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y & =3-6x \\\\ 3y & =3(1-2x) \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & =\\frac{3(1-2x)}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}(1-2x)}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ y & =1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
